    "role": "One portal address first delivers the lantern, then delivers its movable return floor.",
    "solution": "Send the lantern alone through the floor mouth. Relocate the receiving mouth to the west ceiling so its old wall stops the belt on the pressure plate. Ride the lift and cross the high mail gallery to open the receiving niche. Retrieve the lantern and carry it up the east steps to the upper cargo shelf. Move the portal to the high stone outlet, leave the lantern on the shelf and enter that outlet to return to the starting dock. Push the prepared stone down through the same floor mouth. Follow it to the high outlet; the closed weight stop parks it underneath the cargo shelf. Place the lantern on the stone: its weight opens the stop. Dash the stone east, follow underneath, and climb onto it where it parks beneath the final weight. The lantern holds the last door while the stone becomes the stationary final landing.",
    "interconnection": "The first receiving weight opens the upper approach. That approach unlocks its receiving niche. Readdressing the same floor portal delivers the stone under the recovered lantern; pressure opens the stone\u2019s stop, and the travelling lantern reopens that last circuit at the far landing.",
    "novelty": "The portal is deliberately removed from its useful delivery position, then reassigned to transport the architecture that its first parcel needs.",
    "nearestExisting": "B50 closes a receiving mouth into a conveyor stop; F20 readdresses a portal to change which side of a stone is accessible. This is a complete return loop where the same address later supplies a cargo-bearing platform.",
    "superroom": {
      "screenAreas": 3.4434782608695653,
      "stages": [
        "Cargo-only delivery and receiving-stop reversal",
        "High approach and reclaim the parcel",
        "Readdress the portal, send the stone, and ride its cargo circuit"
      ]
    },
    "endingForgiveness": "The stone parks permanently under the final pressure plate. Wide one-way recovery floors below the railway let a late following jump recover without losing its cargo or restarting.",
